-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Submission Envelope
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+A submission contains:
+- visitId: client-generated UUID
+- baseRevisionId: the revision the worker started from, or null for initial creation
+- payload: complete visit record with observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes, plot reference
+- authorId, clientTimestamp, idempotencyKey
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+The server validates plot reference against site-catalog and field types. It stores the submission as an immutable revision with server receipt time and a generated revisionId.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Current State Representation
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+The current state for a visit is the resolved revision chosen by a coordinator, if any. If no resolution exists, the current state is the latest accepted revision by server receipt time, marked as pending review when competing revisions exist.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+Retrieval returns:
+- visitId
+- currentRevisionId
+- currentPayload
+- reviewStatus: resolved | pending | clean
+- competingRevisionIds: list when reviewStatus is pending
+
+## Unresolved Work Representation
+
+Unresolved work exists when two or more revisions share the same baseRevisionId and neither has been resolved. The record store surfaces a revision set:
+- baseRevisionId
+- competingRevisionIds
+- authors and receipt times per revision
+- a boolean indicating whether the visit is export-eligible. Export eligibility is false when reviewStatus is pending.
+
+## Resolution Operation
+
+A coordinator resolves review work by selecting one competing revision as the accepted current revision, or by creating a new manual revision referencing the competing set. The operation records:
+- resolvingCoordinatorId
+- chosenRevisionId or manual payload
+- contributingSubmissionIds
+- resolution timestamp
+
+Resolving a visit clears competing revisions and updates currentRevisionId. Original submissions remain preserved.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent edit policy | Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its base revision; concurrent edits become competing complete revisions for coordinator resolution | Field-aware three-way merge with automatic combination of disjoint field edits | Whole-visit preserves the worker's submitted visit as an intact unit, avoids maintaining merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and prevents automatic combination of scientifically related edits the server cannot infer. It matches the tenet to not silently discard edits and gives coordinators full context for scientific judgment. The trade is a larger review queue, even for unrelated field changes. |
+| Revision identification | Client-generated visitId with server-generated revisionId per submission | Server-generated visitId | Client-generated visitId supports offline creation and retry without server round-trip. |
+
+The field-aware three-way merge alternative remains credible because it would reduce review volume by automatically combining disjoint edits and get more visits export-ready sooner. It requires maintained merge rules for each field type and provenance tracking, and risks combining edits that are scientifically related despite being field-disjoint, e.g., a species ID change paired with a cover estimate change. The team rejected the risk of silent scientific coupling and the ongoing rule maintenance burden for a two-coordinator team.
+
+## Downstream Accommodation
+
+Tablet-sync must retain baseRevisionId for each local draft and send it with every submission. The tablet UI needs to surface pending competing revisions for a visit the worker last edited, showing that their submission is under review and that a newer base exists. Sync state machines must handle server rejection of a submission whose baseRevisionId is already resolved, prompting the worker to rebase their draft onto the current revision.
+
+Review-export must implement a revision comparison screen for coordinators to resolve competing whole-visit revisions. Export eligibility is governed by reviewStatus: visits with pending review are held out of the CSV. The export process must include provenance fields linking an exported row to the resolving coordinator and contributing submission IDs, and must preserve the ability to inspect the original submitted payloads for any exported visit.
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy selected: whole-visit revisions.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Manual revision creation UI and validation rules for coordinators.
+2. Retention policy for superseded revisions.
+
